      "dc.description.abstract": [
        {
          "value": "Users know what they see before where they are: it is more natural to talk about high level visibility information (\"I see such object\") than about one's location or orientation. In this paper we introduce a method to find in 3D worlds a density of viewpoints of camera locations from high level visibility constraints on objects in this world. Our method is based on Rao-Blackwellized importance sampling. For efficiency purposes, the proposal distribution used for sampling is extracted from a visibility preprocessing technique adapted from computer graphics. We apply the method for finding in a 3D city model of Atlanta the virtual locations of real-world cameras and viewpoints.",
          "language": "",
          "authority": null,
          "confidence": -1,
          "place": 0
        }
      ],
